oisture","Lack of oxygen","Excess sunlight"],"B",5,1,"540c8e21dc80"],["A student places a clean iron nail in copper sulphate solution. After some time, a brown layer appears on the nail. This shows that iron:",["Is reduced","Is less reactive than copper","Displaces copper from solution","Prevents oxidation"],"C",5,1,"448f0a87946d"],["During electroplating of a spoon with silver, the spoon is connected to the:",["Anode","Cathode","Salt bridge","Positive terminal only"],"B",5,1,"913b54bd356a"],["A metal object is painted before being used outdoors. The paint mainly:",["Increases conductivity","Prevents contact with air and moisture","Speeds up oxidation","Reduces weight of metal"],"B",5,1,"4fe69711a56e"],["A ship made of iron is protected by attaching blocks of magnesium. Magnesium protects iron because it:",["Is less reactive than iron","Reacts first with oxygen","Prevents water evaporation","Forms alloy with iron"],"B",5,1,"ec549825df0a"],["A student observes that copper metal does not react with zinc sulphate solution. This indicates that copper is:",["More reactive than zinc","Less reactive than zinc","Equal in reactivity to zinc","An oxidizing agent"],"B",5,1,"39f635fd2537"],["During rusting, iron undergoes:",["Reduction","Oxidation","Neutralization","Polymerization"],"B",5,1,"c04c200cb875"],["A silver ring becomes black after long exposure to air. This is an example of:",["Distillation","Corrosion","Neutralization","Electrolysis"],"B",5,1,"897dc40f4809"],["A reaction vessel contains hydrogen gas reacting with copper oxide. If copper oxide changes into copper, copper oxide has:",["Been oxidized","Lost oxygen","Gained electrons from copper","Increased oxidation number"],"B",5,1,"63bd96fbfeea"],["A student accidentally scratches the zinc coating on galvanized iron. The iron still remains protected because zinc:",["Is decorative","Acts as sacrificial metal","Is non-reactive","Absorbs moisture"],"B",5,1,"488157943b0c"],["Which situation best represents oxidation?",["Gain of electrons by chlorine","Removal of oxygen from copper oxide","Loss of electrons by magnesium","Formation of water"],"C",5,1,"ed03119666c3"],["An electroplated object receives metal coating because metal ions:",["Move toward cathode and gain electrons","Move toward anode and lose electrons","React with oxygen","Evaporate during process"],"A",5,1,"7c87a3050358"],["A chemical reaction increases the oxidation number of sulphur from +4 to +6. Sulphur has:",["Been reduced","Been oxidized","Lost oxygen","Gained hydrogen"],"B",5,1,"10091c0b877d"],["A student keeps one iron nail in dry air and another in moist air. Which nail rusts faster?",["Nail in dry air","Nail in moist air","Both equally","Neither rusts"],"B",5,1,"d586d692f857"],["In electrochemistry, the movement of electrons takes place:",["Through solution only","From cathode to anode inside solution","During redox reactions","Only in acids"],"C",5,1,"9f7ba74f5608"],["A laboratory technician coats iron with chromium to make it shiny and corrosion resistant. This process is:",["Rusting","Distillation","Electroplating","Refining"],"C",5,1,"b55004131843"],["Iron pipes buried underground are often attached with magnesium strips. This method is called:",["Galvanization","Electrolysis","Sacrificial protection","Neutralization"],"C",5,1,"e19716e8cf8f"],["A student notices bubbles during an electrochemical experiment. The bubbles indicate:",["Gas formation due to chemical reaction","Melting of metal","Rust prevention","Physical change only"],"A",5,1,"0a3e6f715ac2"],["A student wants to protect an iron bicycle from rusting. Which method is most suitable?",["Heating regularly","Keeping in sunlight","Painting surface","Washing with salt water"],"C",5,1,"4d8e0386d1bf"],["A reaction causes copper ions to gain electrons and form copper metal. Copper ions are:",["Oxidized","Reduced","Neutralized","Corroded"],"B",5,1,"573d3ee44508"],["A student compares rusting in distilled water and salty water. Rusting is faster in salty water because:",["Salt decreases conductivity","Salt increases chemical activity","Salt removes oxygen","Salt prevents oxidation"],"B",5,1,"ae4ba6c1b5db"],["A metal spoon coated with silver becomes shiny because silver:",["Absorbs light","Is highly reflective","Prevents oxidation completely","Removes impurities"],"B",5,1,"de3fd83e1842"],["A chemical engineer wants to prevent corrosion in machinery. Which method is least effective?",["Painting","Oiling","Exposure to moisture","Galvanization"],"C",5,1,"7c062937057c"],["Which statement best explains sacrificial protection?",["Less reactive metal protects more reactive metal","More reactive metal corrodes instead of iron","Iron reacts first with oxygen","Oxygen is removed from atmosphere"],"B",5,1,"c76f723ef9ae"],["A student heats copper oxide with carbon. Copper oxide changes into copper because carbon:",["Oxidizes copper","Acts as reducing agent","Prevents electron transfer","Absorbs copper"],"B",5,1,"72f0876d7526"],["During electroplating of gold on jewelry, gold ions:",["Lose electrons and deposit","Gain electrons and deposit","React with oxygen first","Evaporate from solution"],"B",5,1,"7022995953fc"],["A student notices that aluminium does not corrode quickly despite being reactive. This is because aluminium:",["Is non-metallic","Forms protective oxide layer","Does not react with oxygen","Is less reactive than iron"],"B",5,1,"5132e632e064"],["A student passes hydrogen gas over hot copper oxide and notices formation of reddish-brown copper. Which statement is correct?",["Hydrogen is reduced","Copper oxide is oxidized","Hydrogen acts as reducing agent","Copper gains oxygen"],"C",5,1,"9171c93509bd"],["During electroplating of a bicycle handle with chromium, the handle is connected to the negative terminal of the battery. Why?",["To allow chromium ions to gain electrons","To oxidize chromium metal","To remove electrons from handle","To increase rusting speed"],"A",5,1,"f3933eadd542"],["CuO+CO→Cu+CO₂ carbon monoxide is called a reducing agent because it:",["Gains oxygen","Removes oxygen from CuO","Loses electrons to oxygen only","Changes into copper"],"B",5,1,"bc0cd30c4697"],["In ZnO + C → Zn + CO, carbon acts as a reducing agent because it:",["gains oxygen by removing it from ZnO","loses mass","is a metal","turns into zinc"],"A",5,0,"bc0cd30c4697"],["A copper roof slowly turns green after years in the open air. This is an example of:",["distillation","corrosion","neutralization","electrolysis"],"B",3,1,"897dc40f4809"],["The oxidation number of magnesium in MgO is:",["+2","−2","+1","0"],"A",1,0,"f83868e3a987"],["An iron pipeline buried underground is joined to blocks of zinc. The zinc protects the iron because it:",["is less reactive than iron","corrodes first instead of the iron","stops water reaching the soil","is a non-metal"],"B",5,1,"ec549825df0a"],["The substance that loses electrons in a redox reaction is the:",["oxidizing agent","reducing agent","catalyst","electrolyte"],"B",3,0,"25915bf5f6be"],["A zinc strip is placed in copper sulphate solution and a brown coating forms on it. This shows that zinc:",["is more reactive than copper and displaces it","is less reactive than copper","is reduced","does not react"],"A",3,1,"448f0a87946d"],["The oxidation number of oxygen in H₂O is:",["+2","−2","−1","0"],"B",1,0,"99fea1fa7af1"],["For iron to rust, it must be exposed to:",["oxygen and water","nitrogen only","dry air only","hydrogen"],"A",1,0,"1592ef162740"],["The oxidation number of oxygen in H₂O₂ is:",["0","+1","−1","−3"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c7-oxidation-number"],["The oxidation number of iron in Fe₂O₃ is:",["+1","+5","+3","−3"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c7-oxidation-number"],["The oxidation number of nitrogen in HNO₃ is:",["−5","+7","+3","+5"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c7-oxidation-number"],["The oxidation number of manganese in MnO₂ is:",["+4","+2","+6","−4"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c7-oxidation-number"],["The oxidation number of sulphur in SO₂ is:",["+2","+6","−4","+4"],"D",3,0,"