select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 8, $j$[["A student mixes hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide in a beaker. The beaker becomes warm. This observation indicates that the reaction is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Reversible","Photochemical"],"B",5,1,"b094ac85cd6e"],["During an experiment, a student notices that a reaction vessel becomes colder than its surroundings. The reaction is most likely:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Neutral"],"B",5,1,"439adc82cefb"],["A chemist studies a reaction occurring inside a flask. According to energetics, the reacting chemicals inside the flask represent the:",["Surroundings","Environment","System","Catalyst"],"C",5,1,"31e16d27385b"],["A fuel burns in a furnace and releases large amounts of heat. The sign of ΔH for this process is:",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Infinite"],"C",5,1,"03b08dcfcb7e"],["A student claims that energy is released when chemical bonds are broken. Which statement is correct?",["Student is correct","Bond breaking absorbs energy","Bond breaking and formation both release energy","Energy is not involved in bonding"],"B",5,1,"ed937d89ad31"],["Two reactions occur under identical conditions. Reaction X requires 50 kJ activation energy while Reaction Y requires 120 kJ. Which reaction is expected to occur more readily?",["X","Y","Both equally","Cannot be predicted"],"A",5,1,"f97513b1986e"],["A catalyst is added to a reaction mixture. Which change is expected?",["Increase in ΔH","Increase in activation energy","Faster reaction rate","More products formed than predicted"],"C",5,1,"21019bb5ac38"],["A reaction has ΔH = +75 kJ. Which statement is correct?",["Heat is released","Heat is absorbed","No energy change occurs","Reaction is exothermic"],"B",5,1,"a4b4bc8ee185"],["A student observes that ice melts when left on a laboratory bench. The process is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Oxidative"],"B",5,1,"8b29df0a6aa0"],["A spark is needed to start the combustion of petrol even though combustion is exothermic. The spark provides:",["Enthalpy change","Bond energy","Activation energy","Kinetic energy of products"],"C",5,1,"bf1691c88910"],["A reaction proceeds only when particles collide with sufficient energy and correct orientation. This idea is explained by:",["Atomic theory","Collision theory","Kinetic theory","Molecular theory"],"B",5,1,"0081eb19ab34"],["A student heats calcium carbonate strongly. The decomposition occurs because thermal energy:",["Forms stronger bonds","Supplies activation energy","Reduces mass","Increases pressure only"],"B",5,1,"15a14b25e377"],["A reaction pathway changes after adding a catalyst. However, the total energy released remains unchanged. This shows that catalysts:",["Change ΔH","Lower activation energy only","Increase product mass","Alter reactants permanently"],"B",5,1,"045755d14d8f"],["A chemical engineer wants to maximize the number of effective collisions. Which factor is most important?",["Color of reactants","Correct orientation of particles","Shape of container","Type of thermometer used"],"B",5,1,"355828a0ec3a"],["A student compares two reactions. One has ΔH = –150 kJ and the other ΔH = –50 kJ. Which releases more heat?",["First reaction","Second reaction","Both equal","Cannot be compared"],"A",5,1,"8ffc8fdf1299"],["In a laboratory, ammonium nitrate dissolves in water and the solution becomes cold. This indicates that:",["Heat is released","Heat is absorbed","No energy transfer occurs","Catalyst is present"],"B",5,1,"ff386a5777d8"],["A reaction does not occur despite frequent collisions between particles. The most likely reason is:",["Collisions lack sufficient energy","Reactants have mass","Products are unstable","System is closed"],"A",5,1,"374564a6d0c2"],["A scientist records a negative ΔH value for a reaction. This means:",["Products contain more energy than reactants","Heat is absorbed","Heat is released","Activation energy is zero"],"C",5,1,"474dadaaab48"],["A student performs an experiment inside a calorimeter. The calorimeter itself is considered:",["System","Product","Surroundings","Reactant"],"C",5,1,"34ed917afe1f"],["Which process requires continuous absorption of energy?",["Freezing water","Condensation of steam","Melting ice","Formation of water from hydrogen and oxygen"],"C",5,1,"39cada73e792"],["A reaction releases 250 kJ of energy. Which value best represents ΔH?",["+250 kJ","–250 kJ","0 kJ","+125 kJ"],"B",5,1,"9a233a42b1b0"],["A catalyst is recovered unchanged after a reaction. This demonstrates that catalysts:",["Are reactants","Are consumed completely","Remain chemically unchanged","Become products"],"C",5,1,"e26031d472f7"],["A reaction with a very high activation energy is usually:",["Faster","Slower","More exothermic","More reversible"],"B",5,1,"37fe3ac04a81"],["When hydrogen burns in oxygen, water is formed and heat is released. Which statement is correct?",["Bond breaking releases energy only","Bond formation releases energy","No bonds are formed","Heat is absorbed overall"],"B",5,1,"122e5e669a14"],["A student accidentally removes the catalyst from an industrial process. The most likely consequence is:",["Reaction stops completely","Reaction becomes faster","Reaction becomes slower","ΔH becomes positive"],"C",5,1,"c6822fd575ec"],["Which observation provides direct evidence that a reaction is exothermic?",["Temperature of surroundings increases","Temperature decreases","Color changes","Gas is produced"],"A",5,1,"88add2202c6f"],["A reaction absorbs 120 kJ from surroundings. The products will generally possess:",["Less energy than reactants","Equal energy to reactants","More energy than reactants","No measurable energy"],"C",5,1,"afd32a2efe02"],["A student observes many collisions but very little product formation. Which factor is most likely missing?",["Correct orientation","Reactants","Container","Mass"],"A",5,1,"ac8b3bc829cd"],["Which situation best represents an activated complex?",["Stable product","Highly unstable intermediate state","Catalyst particle","Final reactant mixture"],"B",5,1,"7d456a4954e0"],["An industrial chemist wants to increase production without changing ΔH. The best option is:",["Add catalyst","Change product formula","Increase product mass","Change reaction type"],"A",5,1,"ec0295d976d8"],["A reaction profile diagram shows a very tall energy barrier. This barrier represents:",["ΔH","Potential energy of products","Activation energy","Heat released"],"C",5,1,"f1d429081b62"],["A process releases heat while converting liquid water into ice. The process is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Catalytic","Neutral"],"B",5,1,"5f31cbf100ec"],["A student touches the outside of a reaction vessel and finds it hot. Which conclusion is valid?",["Reaction absorbs heat","Heat flows from surroundings to system","Heat flows from system to surroundings","No energy transfer occurs"],"C",5,1,"7ec987b9f606"],["Why is a matchstick rubbed before it ignites?",["To decrease activation energy","To supply activation energy","To increase ΔH","To reduce bond energy"],"B",5,1,"4b059574dc80"],["A reaction proceeds rapidly after catalyst addition because:",["More products are created instantly","Activation energy decreases","ΔH becomes zero","Bond energies increase"],"B",5,1,"56fd44fafe22"],["During respiration, glucose reacts with oxygen. The released energy is mainly used for:",["Producing catalysts","Life processes","Destroying enzymes","Increasing atomic mass"],"B",5,1,"057852d92c7d"],["A student compares two exothermic reactions. Reaction A has ΔH = –80 kJ and Reaction B has ΔH = –200 kJ. Which releases more energy?",["Reaction A","Reaction B","Both equal","Cannot determine"],"B",5,1,"c0cbd8097474"],["The decomposition of limestone requires continuous heating. Therefore, the reaction is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Catalytic","Neutralization"],"B",5,1,"c83aef2f4d20"],["A catalyst provides an alternative pathway that is:",["Longer and slower","Higher in energy","Lower in activation energy","Independent of reactants"],"C",5,1,"cc613ee7e6d5"],["A student records a positive ΔH value. Which statement is true?",["Heat leaves the system","Heat enters the system","Reaction is exothermic","Catalyst is absent"],"B",5,1,"b71853a99963"],["Which situation best illustrates energy absorption during bond breaking?",["Formation of H₂O from H₂ and O₂","Ignition of methane","Separation of atoms in a molecule","Freezing of water"],"C",5,1,"9aea7d78528c"],["An effective collision occurs when particles:",["Move slowly","Have sufficient energy and proper orientation","Are large in size","Contain catalyst atoms"],"B",5,1,"c3ab6e09622d"],["Which process is most likely to have a positive ΔH?",["Condensation of steam","Freezing water","Burning coal","Thermal decomposition of limestone"],"D",5,1,"d290aa3c125e"],["A catalyst is present in a reaction vessel. At the end of the experiment, its mass remains unchanged. This confirms that catalysts:",["Are consumed and regenerated","Remain unchanged overall","Become products","Are destroyed slowly"],"B",5,1,"4e735afb7ba6"],["A student wants to identify an exothermic reaction. Which evidence is most reliable?",["Heat is evolved","Gas is formed","Color changes","Precipitate forms"],"A",5,1,"7f01abffe4bf"],["A reaction has low activation energy. Under identical conditions, it will generally:",["Proceed more easily","Require more catalyst","Absorb more heat","Have positive ΔH"],"A",5,1,"3241d39447a1"],["In a reaction profile, the difference between reactant energy and peak energy represents:",["ΔH","Activation energy","Heat of formation","Bond energy"],"B",5,1,"cffdd74d92d0"],["A chemical reaction releases energy because:",["More energy is absorbed in bond breaking than released in bond formation","Energy changes are random","More energy is released in bond formation than absorbed in bond breaking","No bonds are involved"],"C",5,1,"f013085fe5c5"],["A student is asked to identify the main purpose of studying chemical energetics. The best answer is:",["To calculate atomic masses only","To understand energy changes in chemical reactions","To classify elements only","To determine molecular shapes only"],"B",5,1,"ceeae7b128ee"],["The study of energy changes during chemical reactions is called:",["Electrochemistry","Thermochemistry","Chemical Energetics","Organic Chemistry"],"C",1,0,"6bda3f9f7387"],["In a chemical reaction, energy is exchanged between:",["Atoms and molecules","System and surroundings","Reactants and catalyst","Products and catalyst"],"B",1,0,"3892301559cc"],["The part of the universe under study is called:",["Environment","Surroundings","System","Medium"],"C",1,0,"3a7b1feb2f86"],["Everything outside the system is called:",["Catalyst","Medium","Reactant","Surroundings"],"D",1,0,"7bfc37971c8d"],["Breaking a chemical bond always:",["Releases energy","Consumes energy","Produces light only","Produces sound only"],"B",1,0,"6ecf69390c57"],["Formation of a chemical bond always:",["Absorbs energy","Consumes heat","Releases energy","Stops reaction"],"C",1,0,"d756f6aad0b0"],["A reaction that releases heat to the surroundings is:",["Endothermic","Neutral","Reversible","Exothermic"],"D",1,0,"59e57d6cdaf9"],["Combustion of methane is an example of:",["Endothermic reaction","Exothermic reaction","Decomposition reaction","Neutralization reaction"],"B",1,0,"e5f5ce17c280"],["In an exothermic reaction, heat flows:",["From surroundings to system","From system to surroundings","Into catalyst only","Into reactants only"],"B",1,0,"c0ca7a5a549b"],["A reaction that absorbs heat from its surroundings is:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Displacement","Redox"],"B",1,0,"c3cb53007819"],["Which process is endothermic?",["Freezing water","Condensation of steam","Sublimation of dry ice","Combustion of fuel"],"C",1,0,"dac93682d003"],["The thermal energy evolved or absorbed during a reaction is called:",["Entropy","Activation energy","Enthalpy of reaction","Kinetic energy"],"C",1,0,"0dc363ec040f"],["The symbol used for enthalpy change is:",["ΔE","ΔH","ΔG","ΔS"],"B",1,0,"542c4432e0c2"],["For an exothermic reaction, ΔH is:",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Infinite"],"C",1,0,"d6cdfe81d58f"],["For an endothermic reaction, ΔH is:",["Negative","Positive","Zero","Undefined"],"B",1,0,"1b3a2a44960c"],["Standard enthalpy change is measured at:",["0 K","100°C","298 K","500 K"],"C",1,0,"eff3c383cea5"],["The energy required to break one mole of a bond is called:",["Bond formation energy","Activation energy","Bond dissociation energy","Heat of combustion"],"C",1,0,"e0e0d4c747b9"],["The energy released when one mole of bonds is formed is:",["Bond formation energy","Activation energy","Ionization energy","Bond dissociation energy"],"A",1,0,"64e2ce73de80"],["The enthalpy change of a reaction depends upon:",["Bond energies involved","Color of reactants","Shape of container","Pressure only"],"A",1,0,"46468f8c6a0b"],["A reaction with ΔH = –393.5 kJ is:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Reversible","Slow"],"B",1,0,"464be1c64848"],["The minimum energy required for effective collision is:",["Potential energy","Enthalpy","Activation energy","Nuclear energy"],"C",1,0,"73d291448efa"],["A reaction will occur only when particles:",["Have mass","Possess activation energy","Are colored","Are liquids"],"B",1,0,"5b7230a9796a"],["According to collision theory, a reaction occurs when particles:",["Dissolve completely","Collide effectively","Become ions","Change color"],"B",1,0,"9b3fdbfb362f"],["An effective collision must have:",["Proper orientation and sufficient energy","High pressure only","Large molecules only","Catalyst only"],"A",1,0,"32125fc6a649"],["If reacting particles possess energy less than activation energy:",["Reaction speeds up","Reaction becomes exothermic","No reaction occurs","Products form instantly"],"C",1,0,"b42c20f6cd6a"],["Higher activation energy generally means:",["Faster reaction","Slower reaction","No effect on rate","More products"],"B",1,0,"bbf4911f66fd"],["A catalyst increases reaction rate by:",["Increasing ΔH","Raising temperature permanently","Lowering activation energy","Increasing product mass"],"C",1,0,"078b9fc35eea"],["A catalyst remains:",["Consumed completely","Chemically unchanged","Converted into product","Burnt away"],"B",1,0,"ca56aa400d9b"],["The phenomenon of using a catalyst is called:",["Catalysis","Neutralization","Distillation","Sublimation"],"A",1,0,"a72b1cb8cd4e"],["A catalyst affects:",["Activation energy","Enthalpy change","Mass of products","Nature of reactants"],"A",1,0,"c6ee16420f3b"],["A catalyst does NOT change:",["Reaction pathway","Activation energy","Enthalpy change","Reaction rate"],"C",1,0,"5e8581e965af"],["Enzymes are catalysts found in:",["Metals","Living organisms","Rocks","Fuels"],"B",1,0,"dcd577d2c6f7"],["Respiration is a process in which:",["Energy is absorbed from surroundings","Energy is released from food","Water is produced only","Oxygen is formed"],"B",1,0,"ae87e550f87a"],["Respiration is generally:",["Endothermic","Exothermic","Neutral","Photochemical"],"B",1,0,"5e9c0194a77a"],["The main source of energy in respiration is:",["Nitrogen","Glucose","Carbon dioxide","Water"],"B",1,0,"429a00a4af20"],["Which process releases heat?",["Melting ice","Sublimation of dry ice","Combustion of fuel","Decomposition of limestone"],"C",1,0,"c712ad2a10dd"],["A student studies limestone reacting with hydrochloric acid in a test tube. The reacting chemicals represent the:",["Surroundings","Environment","System","Catalyst"],"C",1,1,"b91626be76a2"],["The test tube and air around it, are considered:",["Reactants","System","Products","Surroundings"],"D",1,0,"0395c2d029d2"],["Which process is exothermic?",["Freezing water","Sublimation of dry ice","Thermal decomposition of limestone","Breaking bonds"],"A",1,0,"ef05e3e50509"],["During an endothermic reaction:",["Heat enters the system","Heat leaves the system","No energy transfer occurs","Catalyst absorbs all heat"],"A",1,1,"e32eb9e52a09"],["The combustion of carbon producing carbon dioxide has:",["Positive ΔH","Neg